---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GasCalculator from '../components/GasCalculator.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<Layout 
    title="Gas Mileage Calculator | Calculate MPG 2026"
    description="Free online gas mileage calculator for 2026. Calculate your exact MPG (miles per gallon) to track your car's fuel efficiency and save money at the pump."
    keywords="gas mileage calculator, car mileage calculator, calculate mpg, fuel efficiency calculator, miles per gallon calculator"
>
    <section class="hero">
        <h1>MILEAGE CALCULATOR</h1>
        
        <div class="instruction-box">
            <p>Track your car or bike's fuel efficiency with ease! This mileage calculator helps you determine mileage for any vehicle, including cars, SUVs, trucks, taxis, and motorcycles. Simply enter the distance travelled, fuel used, and fuel price per unit. Get results in various units like kilometres per liter (kmpl) or miles per gallon (mpg). Our fuel mileage calculator supports petrol, diesel, and CNG for a comprehensive picture. The fuel efficiency figures are generally in mpg or kmpl but you can also select kmpg (kilometre per gallon) or mpl (miles per litre) to calculate the mileage in different units.</p>
        </div>

        <GasCalculator />
    </section>

    <section class="seo-section">
        <div class="seo-content">
            <h2>The Best Free Gas Mileage Calculator for 2026</h2>
            <p>Welcome to our comprehensive <strong>gas mileage calculator</strong>. With fuel prices constantly fluctuating, knowing your exact <strong>miles per gallon (MPG)</strong> is crucial for budgeting and saving money. Our tool makes it simple to track your vehicle's fuel efficiency.</p>
            
            <h3>How to Calculate Gas Mileage (MPG)</h3>
            <p>Our calculator makes it incredibly easy to find out how many miles per gallon your vehicle is actually getting. Simply input your starting odometer reading when you fill up, your ending odometer reading at your next fill-up, and the number of gallons added. The formula used is simple: <em>(Ending Odometer - Starting Odometer) ÷ Gallons Added</em>.</p>
            
            <h3>Why Track Your MPG?</h3>
            <p>Tracking your gas mileage over time can alert you to potential maintenance issues. If your MPG suddenly drops, it might be time for an oil change, new spark plugs, or to check your tire pressure. Improving your MPG not only saves you money but is also better for the environment.</p>

            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3>What is a good gas mileage?</h3>
                <p>A "good" gas mileage depends on the type of vehicle. For compact cars and sedans, 25-30 MPG in the city and 30-40 MPG on the highway is considered good. For SUVs and trucks, 20-25 MPG is more common. Hybrids and EVs can achieve the equivalent of 50+ MPG.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h3>Why is my car's gas mileage decreasing?</h3>
                <p>A drop in fuel efficiency can be caused by several factors: underinflated tires, dirty air filters, faulty spark plugs, carrying excess weight, or aggressive driving habits. Regular maintenance is the best way to preserve your vehicle's MPG.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
